  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "Hbol",
      "code_hash": "675e8d864d84c1e8ec11042e68a9544c",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"optimal-bayesian-ranking\">optimal bayesian ranking</h1>\n<h3 align='center'>chapter 1: bernoulli to beta</h3>\n<p align='center'>by miraia s. chiou \u00a9 2024</span>\n\n<h2 id=\"introduction\">introduction</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>fuz</strong> can be used to improve on basic bayesian ranking by taking into account the shape of distributions rather than just the means. the optimal method is to use mode-parameterized beta or dirichlet distributions, but using a prior obtained by multiplicative pooling also improves upon traditional <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayesian_average\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">bayesian averaging/ranking</a></span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">if you know already know how to derive the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rule_of_succession\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">rule of succession</a>, skip to <a href=\"#claims\">claims</a>. note that this chapter covers known principles. the next chapters cover research that could be considered novel (disclaimer: i am an amateur in this field).</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "MJUe",
      "code_hash": "4f281a65e87c7022384e801013bc8369",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"the-bernoulli-distribution\">the bernoulli distribution</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">to understand optimal bayesian ranking, start with the bernoulli distribution. this can be thought of as coin flips, upvotes and downvotes, yes/no ratings, or anything representable as binary. as an example the video game service <em>steam</em> uses yes/no (thumbs up or thumbs down). in this case, a game with 6 thumbs up and 2 thumbs down has a bernoulli score distribution with <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(p=0.75||)</marimo-tex>.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "PKri",
      "code_hash": "0b7f72dafab3684500dccceda4792802",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">this distribution represents the sample mean. however, we have some additional information we want to represent - the number of ratings (count). we can do this with the binomial distribution.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "Xref",
      "code_hash": "838f84b6e60efe55563bd1f8dce07848",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"bernoulli-to-binomial\">bernoulli to binomial</h2></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "Hstk",
      "code_hash": "08abb4204b6dd4bb92c19765052a5551",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"binomial-to-beta\">binomial to beta</h2></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ROlb",
      "code_hash": "96bfe32ace4c72de98725a23bfbc3031",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">what do you notice here?</span>\n<ol>\n<li>this is not a probability distribution function (pdf) yet. to make it a pdf, we'll divide the probability by the integral to get the density.</li>\n<li>the mode (peak) is <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(p||)</marimo-tex>.</li>\n<li>the mean of the possible true means is different from the mode.</li>\n<li>after turning this into a pdf, this is a beta distribution.</li>\n</ol>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">let's turn this into a pdf and see which beta distributions might match. fortunately <strong>fuz</strong> allows you to make beta distributions in a variety of ways.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ulZA",
      "code_hash": "17f1a2be16c5f8c80cf4130814f41cbf",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">from the chart, it's clear that the beta distribution parameterized by mode and number of trials matches the binomial-derived pdf.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">what are the implications? moving from bernoulli to binomial to beta is a well-known fundamental concept in statistics, so what's new here? let's set things up to understand.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ecfG",
      "code_hash": "a6831ebb38233ffd772fcdd66d31c34f",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"setup\">setup</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">first, for the purposes of this chapter, let's define:</span>\n<marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mu_s &amp;= \\text{sample mean} \\\\\n\\mu_\\top &amp;= \\text{the true mean} \\\\\n\\mu_\\diamond &amp;= \\text{a possible true mean} \\\\\n\\Mu &amp;= \\text{the distribution of possible true means} \\\\\n\\mathrm{E}[M] = \\mu_\\Mu &amp;= \\text{the mean of possible true means} \\\\\n\\hat{\\Mu} &amp;= \\text{the mode of the possible true mean distribution} \\\\\n\\hat{\\Beta} &amp;= \\text{the mode of a beta distribution} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n||]</marimo-tex><span class=\"paragraph\">in addition, we use standard notation for the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beta_distribution\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">beta distribution from wikipedia</a>.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">here are some insights:</span>\n<ol>\n<li>the parameterization of the beta by mode and no. of trials <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(t||)</marimo-tex>, where <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(t = k-2||)</marimo-tex>, matches best.</li>\n<li>the mean of the beta <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\mu_B||)</marimo-tex> corresponds to the mean of possible true means <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\mu_\\Mu||)</marimo-tex></li>\n</ol></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "Pvdt",
      "code_hash": "98d8aeaf05c64b0acb794721a3894ef9",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"the-mean-of-possible-true-means\">the mean of possible true means</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">first, what's the best way to calculate <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\mu_\\Mu||)</marimo-tex>? just a bit of algebra:</span>\n<marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &amp;= k-2 \\\\\nk &amp;= \\alpha + \\beta \\\\\n\\hat{\\Beta} &amp;\\coloneqq \\mu_s\\\\\n\\hat{\\Beta} &amp;= \\frac{\\alpha-1}{\\alpha+\\beta-2} = \\frac{\\alpha-1}{t} \\\\\n\\Mu &amp;= \\Beta(\\alpha,\\beta) \\\\\n\\mu_\\Mu &amp;= \\frac{\\alpha}{k} \\\\\n\\alpha &amp;= k\\mu_\\Mu = t\\hat{\\Beta} + 1 \\\\\n\\mu_\\Mu &amp;= \\frac{t \\mu_s + 1}{k} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n||]</marimo-tex><span class=\"paragraph\">thus, to find <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\mu_\\Mu||)</marimo-tex> given number of ratings <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(t||)</marimo-tex> and mean <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\mu_s||)</marimo-tex>:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "aLJB",
      "code_hash": "b020be39e0c2a32d44d2649646cebafc",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">we have just derived the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rule_of_succession\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">rule of succession</a>, aka the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_distribution#Estimation_of_parameters\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">bayes estimator</a> given a uniform prior. from here on i will refer to this estimator as <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\mu_\\Mu||)</marimo-tex> to reflect its meaning in the context of bayesian ranking.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nHfw",
      "code_hash": "13912fc795aef9fbdb0756b346e60186",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"claims\">claims</h2></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "AjVT",
      "code_hash": "30efd1a607fa77b2232df8618b0c42c9",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">i will address these claims in the following chapters.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">although the rule of succession has been known since the 18th century, i believe this is a novel angle to look at the problem of bayesian ranking.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
